EXT. PLATTE RIVER FERRY - FLOOD STAGE - DAWN

A flat barge on a rope. Brown water, fast and wide. Mist hangs on the cottonwoods. The rope sings under the strain.

SHERIFF AMOS DRUMMOND, 50s, iron-grey moustache, badge dull with rust, stands at the rail with a shotgun. CALEB DUNMORE, 30s, wrists chained, hat gone, a cut over one eye, sits on a crate. SILAS PRUITT, 70, one milky eye, ferryman, leans on the long pole and hums.

The barge lurches. Drummond steadies. Dunmore grins.

DUNMORE
Rope's fraying, Sheriff.

DRUMMOND
Shut your mouth.

PRUITT
He's right.

DRUMMOND
Whose side are you on, Silas?

PRUITT
The side that stays dry.

Drummond swings the shotgun toward Pruitt. Pruitt does not blink.

DRUMMOND
Cut the rope and I cut you.

PRUITT
Then who poles?

DUNMORE
Let me pole. I grew up on this river.

DRUMMOND
You grew up in a jail.

DUNMORE
After the river.

Drummond steps close. Dunmore stares up at him.

DRUMMOND
Talk.

DUNMORE
My brother's buried in Kearney. Left me a map. Gold under the old ferry landing, before the flood took it. I tell you, you let me go.

DRUMMOND
No.

DUNMORE
Half. You and me. Silas gets the barge.

PRUITT
I got a barge.

DUNMORE
A better one.

DRUMMOND
You're lying.

DUNMORE
Maybe. But what's a lie when the rope's about to snap?

PRUITT
Sheriff.

DRUMMOND
Not now, Silas.

PRUITT
Sheriff, look downriver.

A tree trunk the size of a church bell tower tumbles out of the mist, root-wad first, straight for the barge.

DRUMMOND
Pole! Pole!

Pruitt jams the pole. Dunmore lunges for the key on Drummond's belt. Drummond clubs him with the shotgun stock. Dunmore drops. The trunk clips the barge. Planks scream.

Drummond is on his knees. Dunmore is on his back. Pruitt hangs from the pole, laughing, wild.

PRUITT
That's the river!

The rope snaps. The barge spins free into the brown.
